99% of all species that have ever existed are extinct.  The losers are
fossilized under your feet!  Now, it is true that many species don't
come into direct competition with each other (and thus, both can
survive), and in many cases, the interaction between two species is
mutually beneficial.  But the vast bulk of species have perished.

The interesting question is to examine what the relationship between
two nations (or ethnic groups) is in any particular case.  For
instance, the Romans and Carthaginians were in direct competition; the
Carthaginians were exterminated, both genetically and memetically.
The US and Western Europe seems to have settled into some sort of
mutually beneficial system.  The US and Japan situation still seems to
be in a situation where the prize is domination of the other.

But, as I said before, the introduction of new forms of evolution does
not eliminate old ones.  If some nation somehow manages to kill off
all the others, it will be the ancestors of all humans afterward.  If
it is possible for nations to occasionally achieve that atrocity,
nations that do not will leave no descendants.

Dale

