Subject: Tailoring Memes to Win Envirnmentalists

   From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu

   Tribal society was the only time where the "love it, or leave it"
   philosophy worked. If you didn't like the government, you could vote
   with your feet. Now the majority of land on the earth is owned
   by some government.

It seems like this is an important issue that's gotten a lot less
study than it deserves.  You could certainly vote with your feet in
tribal society, but it was hard to find another tribe to admit you --
tribes tend to run on networks of reciprocal obligations, and don't
like strangers.  In modern times, you could usually leave a country
you didn't like (the Communist bloc was pretty unique in its efforts
to keep people from leaving), but it's hard to get into a country that
would be pleasant to live in (e.g., the US).  Usually you can get into
unpleasant countries fairly easily (e.g., the USSR and most poor
countries), unless you're from a country that lots of peole want to
leave.

The trouble is that almost all habitable land is covered by human
societies, so it's hard to escape the common problems of human
societies.

Dale

