From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Species Monopoly

The recent forest summit here in Oregon also revealed how government 
interference is causing habitat destruction.  Lumber is a very long-range 
investment, often 50 years or more.   Given world demographic
trends, and the consequences for world lumber supply & demand, one 
can be pretty confident that lumber prices will increase faster than 
interest rates over the next several decades.  Add in the growth of the 
trees themselves, and you get a very nice payback from just sitting 
back and letting these primitive nanoassemblers do their stuff, and 
letting the owls et. al. play in them.  But the investor has to have 
confidence that he will own the lumber in 50 years, or can sell the stand 
while it's still valuable. 

If this confidence erodes, it pays to log the land immediately,
while the owner can still extract some value from it.  The recent
spate of regulations, most infamously those dealing with the spotted
owl, have eroded the confidence of private landowners' ability to
control their own stands.  As a result, private landowners
anticipating regulations are lumbering off land _because it is
good habitat_!

Add this to the subsidized road-building on state & federal lands
(govn't owns about 3/4 of the timber land out here), and the lack
of market incentives for replanting or otherwise caring for 
government land, and it is probable the bulk of habitat destruction 
is due to government interference, not normal market forces.



