First I would like to commend Dean Tribble for one of the few practical
"how-to" postings on this thread.

	From: tribble@memex.com (E. Dean Tribble)

	A fun heuristic: Fredkin's paradox (poorly phrased, I'm
	afraid): When choosing between 2 options, if the options are
	clearly of radically different value, then the choice is easy.
	It's when the options have approximately the same apparent
	value that we spend lots of time wrestling over the choice.
	Thje cases where we spend lots of effort deciding, however
	precisely those cases in which the difference in value between
	one choice and the other is smallest.

	Once I heard that, I immediately started recognizing when I was
	spending lots of time on a tradeoff, and could quickly decide
	whether it was because the tradeoff was so close that in fact
	it didn't make any difference.  As a result, many decisions
	that used to be hard and take a long time now go very quickly,
	and I spend my decision capacity on decisions that are
	important.

Compare this with the following excerpt:

	Moreover, Rosch found borderline members [fuzzy classes] seemed
	to cause more uncertainty.  In a follow-up, she asked subjects
	to respond true or false to assertions such as "A carrot is a
	vegetable" and "A pickle is a vegetable."  She found they
	answered true significantly faster with high-ranking items like
	~carrot~ than low-ranking ones like ~pickle~.  The marginal
	examples demanded more thought.  Like Siskel and Ebert's so-so
	movies, they seemed harder to round off.

This excerpt is from a newly published book I would highly recommend to
members of this list:

McNeill, Daniel, and Freiberger, Paul, _Fuzzy Logic_, Simon&Schuster, 1993,
p.85.

The Rosch refered to in this excerpt is Eleanor Rosch, a psychologist
at UC Berkeley.  Her experiment was performed in 1973.  I'm curious as
to the origins of this "Fredkin's Paradox" that Dean Tribble refers to.

There are many other "connections" in this book with the discussions I
hear going on in adjacent (concurrent?) threads on this list.  If only
for the extensive bibliography alone the book seems worth it.

