Date: Thu, 08 Apr 93 06:45:03 GMT
From: Michael Clive Price <price@price.demon.co.uk>
Subject: FTL: traversable wormholes (was HUMOR: UFO's from future?)

Ha Ha!  At last, a prefix that not even Tim May can object to :-)

In response to Eric Raymond's heartfelt plea for info- :-)

First, Phil Fraeling asks, about FTL travel through wormholes,:

> Also, I want to know: is this easy ftl travel or more "build a
> cylinder of neutronium 1000 km long and 10 km wide" stuff?

Definitely not easy.  The plan Visser sketches [1] of a traversable
wormhole requires cramming negative energy into the high-tension edges
of a cube (or any polyhedron).  So much negative energy that a *white*
hole forms.  To build a metre cube wormhole requires about -2.10^27 Kg. 
The two cubes (the mouths of the wormhole) are identified with each
other - it has no inside.  Looking at one cube you would see through it
the view from the other cube.  To travel through the wormhole and come
out the other end you fly between the edges, ie into the a face, of the
cube.  In theory you should experience no stresses etc (except worry!)
:-)

Assuming boundless optimism and ignoring the technical challenges of
such a construction.  We have a wormhole constructed.  The peculiar
thing about wormholes is that not only do they enable transport through
space, but time as well.  If we place clocks at each mouth and accelerate
one of the mouths to distant place, the clocks are still in synch with
each other.  What this means is that if you compare the two clock times
(by traversing the wormhole!) they will always agree - even if one mouth
has been time dilated by motion (or the presence of a gravitational
field).

The possibilities for time travel exist if we give the wormhole mouths
the twin paradox treatment.  One mouth is sent at near light speeds to
Alpha Centauri and back.  It experiences total time-dilation of about
8 years (say).  A traveller stepping through the stay-at-home end would
appear at the gone-away-and-come-back end 8 years in the future. 
Travelling in the opposite direction, from the gone-away-and-come-back
end to the stay-at-home end, would take the traveller *back* in time 8
years.  What Visser shows [2] is that this can never happen because you
can't bring the two ends of a wormhole back together again.  A force
builds up between two ends that is mutually repulsive OR the wormhole
collapses.  Either way they can not be bought back together once a time-
shift has been induced between the two ends.  This chronology protection
effect prevents the formation of closed causal loops, but doesn't
prevent you using the wormhole as a FTL gateway.  So you could travel
to Alpha Centauri light years 4 years in our future and then travel back
to here and now.  Or if the wormhole travelled to Alpha at non-
relativistic speeds they would function as a plain old FTL transporter.

These FTL wormholes have some very weird properties, though.  They are
not just simple doorways through hyperspace.  Suppose our civilisation
develops wormhole travel and starts to explore the universe.  The
wormholes at the leading edge of expansion experience profound dilation. 
The stay-at-home viewer sees the wormhole travelling at superluminal
speeds.  At a one gee acceleration (fuelled from the stay-at-home end
or by an intergalactic Bussard ramscoop) the wormhole would enable
travel to the centre of the Milky Way within 21 stay-at-home years and
the Andromeda galaxy within 25 years.  The edge of the observable
universe is reached in within a century or two.  [I'll recheck these 
numbers tonight, I'm going from decades old memory, but they're within 
an order of magnitude]

The radiation of wormholes from the centre (Earth) is imposing a local,
stay-at-home time on its future light cone.  Once imposed such a local
or 'empire time' (as Robin Hanson and I have dubbed it) becomes
increasingly harder to break or redefine.  The centre of each
civilisation defines the apex to the empire-time cone.  Wormhole travel
takes you anywhere/anywhen on the simultaneity light cone.  

We would encounter alien civilisations that are separated from us by
millions/billions of light years and millions/billions of years (as
measured in the co-moving time frame of the universe) within a very
short period of empire time.  When the simultaneity light cones of two
spacefaring civilisations join up the local empire times defined by each
merge.  All of their simultaneity cone becomes available to us and vice
versa (assuming co-operation!).  Interestingly all civilisations would
make contact with each other within the same era of their own empire
time.  Something which I find staggering.  Is the universe trying to 
ensure that each civilisation gets an equal crack at the whip, or what?
How jolly decent!

In a previous post I argued, on statistical grounds, that the nearest
aliens would be over 100 million light years away.  Now it seems that
even if they are a long way away we may be trading with them (for we
would all be meeting at approximately the same stage of development and
so presumably have trading interests in each other) in centuries, rather
than billions of years!

The future is closer than we thought.  Remember, you heard it first on
extropians!

